WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.912
Welcome to Module 12 of the Non-Person
Identity learning path: migrate a

2
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:11.675
service account, from a user account to
a G M S A, a D M S A or Microsoft Entra.

3
00:00:11.675 --> 00:00:14.731
It teaches the companion
guide Service Accounts,

4
00:00:14.731 --> 00:00:17.638
with Chapters 6 and 5 of
the developer workbook.

5
00:00:17.638 --> 00:00:20.564
The module takes about sixty-five minutes,

6
00:00:20.564 --> 00:00:24.180
with a lab on fixtures and
a lab for a test domain.

7
00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.680
Why does this module exist?

8
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.624
A service account's password
is the one nobody changes,

9
00:00:32.624 --> 00:00:34.717
because changing it breaks something.

10
00:00:34.717 --> 00:00:39.318
Modules 7 and 8 taught the kit's
plans for converting those accounts.

11
00:00:39.318 --> 00:00:43.773
This module takes one account at a time
and walks it through each move by hand,

12
00:00:43.773 --> 00:00:45.895
with Microsoft Learn's own commands,

13
00:00:45.895 --> 00:00:49.006
so you know what the plans
send before you approve one.

14
00:00:49.006 --> 00:00:53.592
There are three moves: a user account
to a group managed service account,

15
00:00:53.592 --> 00:00:58.568
the same account to a Windows Server
2025 delegated managed service account,

16
00:00:58.568 --> 00:01:02.066
and an account to a Microsoft
Entra workload identity,

17
00:01:02.066 --> 00:01:04.943
once the workload moves
or gains a cloud path.

18
00:01:04.943 --> 00:01:07.980
Then you watch the kit plan
the same moves on fixtures.

19
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.605
The module adds no gate of its own:

20
00:01:10.605 --> 00:01:14.960
its moves are Gate G5's, and
its rules are the track's.

21
00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:21.623
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

22
00:01:21.623 --> 00:01:25.671
You'll compare the kinds of service
identity: who holds the credential,

23
00:01:25.671 --> 00:01:28.672
where it runs and how exposed
it is to Kerberoasting.

24
00:01:28.672 --> 00:01:31.725
You'll choose a target with
the guide's decision path,

25
00:01:31.725 --> 00:01:33.784
and name its Chapter 3 disposition.

26
00:01:33.784 --> 00:01:37.408
You'll follow Learn's steps to
a G M S A and to a D M S A,

27
00:01:37.408 --> 00:01:41.960
with the checks before the switch
and the rollback after it.

28
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.897
You'll say what moves
to Microsoft Entra I D,

29
00:01:44.897 --> 00:01:48.085
what can't, and why a
hybrid workload keeps both.

30
00:01:48.085 --> 00:01:51.109
And you'll match each step
to the kit's plan action,

31
00:01:51.109 --> 00:01:54.510
which runs only by its approved
hash and never deletes.

32
00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:57.773
Take Module 3 first, for the dispositions.

33
00:01:57.773 --> 00:02:02.660
Module 8 is recommended: this
module reruns part of its lab.

34
00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:09.247
Unit 1 starts with the
guide's comparison table.

35
00:02:09.247 --> 00:02:14.836
Read it for two things: who manages each
credential, and which kinds speak Kerberos.

36
00:02:14.836 --> 00:02:19.783
A user account used as a service has a
password an administrator sets by hand,

37
00:02:19.783 --> 00:02:23.710
and it's highly exposed to
Kerberoasting when it holds an S P N.

38
00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:29.285
An S M S A and a G M S A have a long
random password that Windows manages;

39
00:02:29.285 --> 00:02:34.861
a G M S A's is computed by the domain
controllers from the K D S root key,

40
00:02:34.861 --> 00:02:38.510
and any host allowed to
retrieve it holds it.

41
00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.124
A D M S A binds the secret to the machine,

42
00:02:41.124 --> 00:02:45.510
and Microsoft Learn says it's
designed against Kerberoasting.

43
00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:50.294
The Microsoft Entra kinds, a managed
identity, an app registration with

44
00:02:50.294 --> 00:02:54.835
a certificate and a federated
credential, speak OAuth, not Kerberos.

45
00:02:54.835 --> 00:02:56.947
So the table makes two things plain.

46
00:02:56.947 --> 00:03:01.022
Nothing in Microsoft Entra I D
speaks Kerberos for a service.

47
00:03:01.022 --> 00:03:05.870
And a G M S A is a better
password, not a different protocol.

48
00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.170
Next in Unit 1 is the decision path.

49
00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:15.953
Answer nine questions in order;
the first yes is the target,

50
00:03:15.953 --> 00:03:19.420
and each target lines up with
a Chapter 3 disposition.

51
00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:23.845
An account that's disabled, unused
or without an owner is retired.

52
00:03:23.845 --> 00:03:28.852
A workload on Azure gets a managed
identity; one in CI/CD gets a federated

53
00:03:28.852 --> 00:03:34.708
credential; one on premises that calls Graph
gets the Arc identity or a certificate.

54
00:03:34.708 --> 00:03:40.101
One that needs Kerberos, with
every host on Windows Server

55
00:03:40.101 --> 00:03:44.709
2025 and no unconstrained
delegation, gets a D M S A.

56
00:03:44.709 --> 00:03:46.282
Otherwise, a G M S A.

57
00:03:46.282 --> 00:03:48.932
The rest keep what they
have, with an end date.

58
00:03:48.932 --> 00:03:52.800
Then the track's rules, which
a hand migration follows too:

59
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:56.916
two named owners and a sponsor;
a reviewed disposition first;

60
00:03:56.916 --> 00:04:02.183
a thirty-day parallel run, then disable,
and never delete during the migration;

61
00:04:02.183 --> 00:04:07.330
no client secrets; no privileged
groups; and changes that are reviewed.

62
00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:14.718
Unit 2 is Migrations A and B, by hand.

63
00:04:14.718 --> 00:04:20.080
In Migration A, a user account to
a G M S A, watch for the order.

64
00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.142
There's one K D S root key for the
forest, and the domain controllers wait

65
00:04:25.142 --> 00:04:30.205
up to ten hours from its creation before
they allow a G M S A to be created.

66
00:04:30.205 --> 00:04:35.005
Then you create the G M S A, install
it and test it on every host.

67
00:04:35.005 --> 00:04:39.530
Then you switch the workload and move
the S P N's in one change window.

68
00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:44.442
After a clean thirty days in parallel,
the old account is disabled and moved.

69
00:04:44.442 --> 00:04:49.463
Migration B, a user account to a D
M S A, starts with Learn's limits:

70
00:04:49.463 --> 00:04:53.893
every machine that uses the
account must support D M S A.

71
00:04:53.893 --> 00:04:57.367
Then you create, start, wait and complete.

72
00:04:57.367 --> 00:05:00.529
Learn says to wait at
least two ticket lifetimes;

73
00:05:00.529 --> 00:05:02.979
the program's thirty days covers four.

74
00:05:02.979 --> 00:05:07.443
Learn's D M S A page says never
to delete the original account.

75
00:05:07.443 --> 00:05:09.686
Then skim the guide's special cases,

76
00:05:09.686 --> 00:05:14.380
and stop on failover clusters and
Microsoft Entra Domain Services.

77
00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:24.330
Unit 3 is Migration C, from Active Directory
to a Microsoft Entra workload identity.

78
00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:27.205
It starts when the
workload moves to Azure,

79
00:05:27.205 --> 00:05:32.380
or gains a call to Graph or an Azure A P I,
and only for what it reaches with OAuth.

80
00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:37.211
Learn's order of preference for an app's
credential is a managed identity whenever

81
00:05:37.211 --> 00:05:41.899
possible; federation where the app doesn't
run on Azure; otherwise a certificate

82
00:05:41.899 --> 00:05:46.517
from a trusted certificate authority, kept
in a vault; and never a client secret.

83
00:05:46.517 --> 00:05:49.095
The guide has four targets, C1 to C4,

84
00:05:49.095 --> 00:05:54.156
and step C5 retires the on-premises
account after the parallel run.

85
00:05:54.156 --> 00:05:55.405
And what can't move?

86
00:05:55.405 --> 00:05:59.102
Learn has no Entra identity
a service can use for

87
00:05:59.102 --> 00:06:02.430
Kerberos or N T L M to
an on-premises resource.

88
00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:05.024
A workload that reaches a file share,

89
00:06:05.024 --> 00:06:09.867
a SQL Server login mapped to a
Windows account, or a Kerberos-only

90
00:06:09.867 --> 00:06:15.055
application that way keeps its G M S A
or D M S A until that resource changes.

91
00:06:15.055 --> 00:06:17.950
That's why a hybrid workload keeps both.

92
00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:22.425
Try it.

93
00:06:22.425 --> 00:06:27.227
Lab 1 reruns parts of the workbook's
Chapter 6 and Chapter 5 labs,

94
00:06:27.227 --> 00:06:31.013
in PowerShell 7, in the
non-person identity folder.

95
00:06:31.013 --> 00:06:35.851
The domain and tenant are fictional,
nothing signs in, and nothing is sent.

96
00:06:35.851 --> 00:06:40.325
Step one makes the runs Chapter
6 reads: Chapter 1's register,

97
00:06:40.325 --> 00:06:43.887
Chapter 2's owners and
Chapter 3's dispositions.

98
00:06:43.887 --> 00:06:47.988
It prints nothing, and no account
moves without its disposition.

99
00:06:47.988 --> 00:06:53.227
Step two runs Learn's G M S A, D M S
A and K D S steps against stand-ins:

100
00:06:53.227 --> 00:06:59.187
seventeen tests, each a fact about
one of Learn's published procedures.

101
00:06:59.187 --> 00:07:00.663
Match them to the guide.

102
00:07:00.663 --> 00:07:06.001
B1 skips Learn's delegated M S A state
step, because it fails as published.

103
00:07:06.001 --> 00:07:09.306
B2 restarts only the service
that uses the account,

104
00:07:09.306 --> 00:07:12.612
where Learn's step restarts
every running service.

105
00:07:12.612 --> 00:07:18.937
A3 adds A E S two fifty-six, which
Learn's G M S A sample leaves out.

106
00:07:18.937 --> 00:07:21.500
A4 is a person's step on each host.

107
00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:26.510
And A7 disables the old account,
where Learn's Entra page deletes it.

108
00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:34.010
Step three checks the Kerberos
accounts and builds the plan.

109
00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:35.522
Nine accounts are in scope.

110
00:07:35.522 --> 00:07:40.807
The plan has ten actions, five of
them writes: one D M S A completion,

111
00:07:40.807 --> 00:07:44.597
two G M S A creations and
two retriever changes.

112
00:07:44.597 --> 00:07:48.860
One write is a person's and four are
the pipeline's, and nothing is sent.

113
00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:54.210
Note the plan's S H A two fifty-six on
the last line: it's what someone approves.

114
00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.860
Look first at K D S root key Ready.

115
00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:58.560
That's the guide's step A2.

116
00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:00.884
The check reads the key's start time,

117
00:08:00.884 --> 00:08:04.010
with a delegated read, and
it never creates a key.

118
00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:07.865
Learn's K D S page says the
forest needs one root key,

119
00:08:07.865 --> 00:08:11.050
and the guide says to create only one.

120
00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:18.037
Step four reads the two
migrations the plan sends.

121
00:08:18.037 --> 00:08:20.712
Create G M S A, for S V C dash intranet,

122
00:08:20.712 --> 00:08:26.347
is the guide's step A3 under the managed
service account standard: a name,

123
00:08:26.347 --> 00:08:29.499
its D N S host name,
the service-account O U,

124
00:08:29.499 --> 00:08:33.701
its one domain local group
of hosts as the retrievers,

125
00:08:33.701 --> 00:08:38.763
A E S two fifty-six, and the thirty-day
interval stated at creation.

126
00:08:38.763 --> 00:08:44.562
It isn't outbound-only, because it takes
over S V C dash intranet's S P N's.

127
00:08:44.562 --> 00:08:49.624
Complete D M S A migration, for
S V C dash H R sync, is step B3,

128
00:08:49.624 --> 00:08:55.474
with the D M S A and the superseded
account's distinguished name.

129
00:08:55.474 --> 00:09:00.290
Its migration started forty days
before, more than the program's thirty.

130
00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:08.277
Step five dry-runs the plan with
its approved hash, as the pipeline.

131
00:09:08.277 --> 00:09:11.697
This is the program's rule
that changes are reviewed:

132
00:09:11.697 --> 00:09:16.152
the write path sends only a plan whose
S H A two fifty-six was approved.

133
00:09:16.152 --> 00:09:22.102
The pipeline's G M S A would send the G M
S A creations and the retriever changes.

134
00:09:22.102 --> 00:09:26.102
The D M S A's completion,
step B3, needs Domain Admins,

135
00:09:26.102 --> 00:09:30.003
so it's a person's step,
and the pipeline refuses it.

136
00:09:30.003 --> 00:09:38.514
M S A dash print oh one is an S M S A, so
it goes to a G M S A, never to a D M S A.

137
00:09:38.514 --> 00:09:41.010
And no action deletes anything.

138
00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:48.898
Step six builds Chapter 5's
conversion plan: that's Migration C.

139
00:09:48.898 --> 00:09:53.522
Step seven reads what each conversion
replaces, and its parallel run.

140
00:09:53.522 --> 00:09:57.505
There are five Active Directory
accounts and three client secrets,

141
00:09:57.505 --> 00:10:01.843
each going to a managed identity, a
federated credential or a certificate,

142
00:10:01.843 --> 00:10:04.972
and the plan holds each to
Learn's order of preference.

143
00:10:04.972 --> 00:10:08.784
Conversion six gives an Azure
Functions app a certificate

144
00:10:08.784 --> 00:10:12.597
where a managed identity is
available, so Chapter 5 flags it.

145
00:10:12.597 --> 00:10:17.073
Conversion one has run forty days on
its user-assigned managed identity,

146
00:10:17.073 --> 00:10:19.610
so its old account shows ready to disable.

147
00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:25.923
That row exercises the parallel run only:
the program's target for S V C dash H R

148
00:10:25.923 --> 00:10:31.835
sync is a D M S A, the completion in step
four, so don't take it through step C5.

149
00:10:31.835 --> 00:10:36.973
Conversion eight's old secret still
signs in on day fifty-nine, so it stays.

150
00:10:36.973 --> 00:10:38.940
Then delete the out folder.

151
00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:47.628
Lab 2 does the moves for real, in
a test domain, never production.

152
00:10:47.628 --> 00:10:49.189
The authors haven't run it.

153
00:10:49.189 --> 00:10:50.440
There are two ways.

154
00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:55.006
By hand, for one account: the
guide's Migration A, steps A1 to A7,

155
00:10:55.006 --> 00:10:59.665
with Learn's commands and the
expected output after each one.

156
00:10:59.665 --> 00:11:04.274
You need a test domain with a K D S
root key older than ten hours, a test

157
00:11:04.274 --> 00:11:09.302
user account with an S P N, and a member
server that runs a test service as it.

158
00:11:09.302 --> 00:11:12.178
Back out with the guide's
rollback for Migration A.

159
00:11:12.178 --> 00:11:15.665
Or through the kit: the
workbook's Chapter 6 Lab 2.

160
00:11:15.665 --> 00:11:18.494
It makes a traditional test account,

161
00:11:18.494 --> 00:11:22.643
a test M S A and the G M S A's
domain local retriever group,

162
00:11:22.643 --> 00:11:26.226
applies the plan as a
person in the test domain,

163
00:11:26.226 --> 00:11:31.790
and runs a D M S A migration to completion
on a Windows Server 2025 domain.

164
00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:33.960
Its step six backs it out.

165
00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:39.097
The federal requirement.

166
00:11:39.097 --> 00:11:42.066
The guide's evidence table
has a row for every step,

167
00:11:42.066 --> 00:11:48.073
and these are the rows this module
practices, in NIST SP 800-53.

168
00:11:48.073 --> 00:11:52.332
Creating the account supports A
C six, Least Privilege, I A five,

169
00:11:52.332 --> 00:11:57.845
Authenticator Management, and C M three,
Configuration Change Control, with the

170
00:11:57.845 --> 00:12:03.609
approved plan, its SailPoint request and
ServiceNow change, and the apply manifest.

171
00:12:03.609 --> 00:12:06.637
Installing and testing supports A C six,

172
00:12:06.637 --> 00:12:10.123
with Test A D Service
Account true on each host.

173
00:12:10.123 --> 00:12:15.434
The parallel run supports A C two,
enhancement three, Disable Accounts.

174
00:12:15.434 --> 00:12:17.927
The cloud identities support I A nine,

175
00:12:17.927 --> 00:12:21.785
Service Identification and
Authentication, tailored in.

176
00:12:21.785 --> 00:12:24.303
And the island supports C M eight,

177
00:12:24.303 --> 00:12:28.685
System Component Inventory,
with Chapter 6's island list.

178
00:12:28.685 --> 00:12:32.792
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers,

179
00:12:32.792 --> 00:12:37.550
and which file supports which is the
program's mapping: design guidance.

180
00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:43.275
Check yourself with two questions.

181
00:12:43.275 --> 00:12:47.337
First: a workload stays on
premises and needs Kerberos.

182
00:12:47.337 --> 00:12:51.173
The domain has a Windows
Server 2025 domain controller,

183
00:12:51.173 --> 00:12:55.926
but one of the two hosts that run the
service is on Windows Server 2022.

184
00:12:55.926 --> 00:12:56.987
What's the target?

185
00:12:56.987 --> 00:12:59.825
The answer is b, a G M S A.

186
00:12:59.825 --> 00:13:05.413
A D M S A needs every host that
uses it on Windows Server 2025.

187
00:13:05.413 --> 00:13:11.501
Second: Learn's Entra page ends a move to
a G M S A by deleting the old account.

188
00:13:11.501 --> 00:13:12.925
What does the program do?

189
00:13:12.925 --> 00:13:14.150
The answer is b.

190
00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:17.968
It disables the account after a
clean thirty-day parallel run,

191
00:13:17.968 --> 00:13:21.712
and deletes it only later,
through the decommission process.

192
00:13:21.712 --> 00:13:24.074
The program follows the stricter reading.

193
00:13:24.074 --> 00:13:26.502
The module page has three more questions,

194
00:13:26.502 --> 00:13:29.360
each linked to the
section that answers it.

195
00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:33.935
To sum up.

196
00:13:33.935 --> 00:13:39.602
You compared the kinds of service identity,
chose targets with the decision path,

197
00:13:39.602 --> 00:13:42.986
and read Learn's steps for
a user account to a G M S A,

198
00:13:42.986 --> 00:13:46.285
a D M S A and a Microsoft
Entra workload identity.

199
00:13:46.285 --> 00:13:51.210
Then you saw the kit plan the same moves:
a G M S A with the standard's settings,

200
00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:54.600
a D M S A migration completed
after its parallel run,

201
00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:59.363
five Active Directory accounts and
three secrets converted in Learn's

202
00:13:59.363 --> 00:14:03.723
order of preference, and every plan
sent only by its approved hash.

203
00:14:03.723 --> 00:14:08.894
The evidence an ISSO keeps for a migration
is the guide's evidence table by step,

204
00:14:08.894 --> 00:14:13.509
with the kit's two plans and their
approved hashes, the apply manifests,

205
00:14:13.509 --> 00:14:18.123
Test A D Service Account on each host,
and the island list for what stays.

206
00:14:18.123 --> 00:14:21.510
Before you move an account, work
through the guide's checklist.

207
00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:25.295
Next, go back to the learning
path, or take Module 11,

208
00:14:25.295 --> 00:14:28.760
Deploy to test, then
production, if you haven't.
